Upstream fixes applied to the bundled iceberg-cpp; the nix package applies
the same patch (see nix/iceberg-cpp/default.nix).

- IcebergThirdpartyToolchain.cmake: reuse an existing avrocpp_s target (from
  Tenzir's avro plugin) instead of fetching and defining it again, and do
  not look up an already-found system avro-cpp package again: its config
  defines ALIAS targets unconditionally and fails the second time.
- Resolve utf8proc after Arrow: a static Arrow package finds utf8proc
  through its own find module, which defines utf8proc::utf8proc
  unconditionally and fails when the target exists already. utf8proc's
  package config skips existing targets.
- Fetch CRoaring's checksum-pinned amalgamation files instead
  of its repository archive containing benchmark data; disable CPM for
  custom CRoaring source archives. Keep system-package discovery intact.
- arrow_s3_file_io.cc: EnsureS3Initialized() fails (and caches the failure)
  when the host application already initialized Arrow's S3 subsystem, which
  Tenzir's s3 plugin always does; treat that as already-initialized.
- partition_summary.cc: PartitionSummary::Update casts every partition value
  to the partition field type, but LiteralCaster::CastTo refuses null
  literals, so any data file with a null partition value fails to commit.
  The stats themselves handle null fine (contains_null); feed it directly.
- url_encoder.cc: uses int8_t without including <cstdint>.
- error_handlers.cc, http_client.cc: a commit POST whose response never
  arrives (connection reset, timeout) surfaced as a generic REST error, so
  Transaction::Commit's Finalize deleted the manifest list it had written
  even though the server may have applied the commit, leaving the committed
  snapshot pointing at a deleted file and the table unreadable. Report the
  no-response case as CommitStateUnknown instead: Finalize then preserves
  the commit artifacts, and callers resolve the unknown outcome against the
  reloaded table (see the commit_uncertain test of the to_iceberg operator).
  Mutating requests (Post, Delete) additionally stop sharing the connection
  pool: when a reused keep-alive connection dies before the response,
  libcurl transparently re-sends the request, so the client sees the
  re-send's 409 instead of the lost response, treats the commit as a plain
  conflict, and the retry's re-Apply deletes manifests the server-side
  committed snapshot references. Fresh connections make a lost response
  surface as the transport error it is.
- manifest_adapter.cc: a partition summary dropped a bound that serializes
  to zero bytes, which is the bound of an empty string or binary value, and
  wrote null instead. Readers take a null lower bound to mean that every
  value is null and skip the manifest, so equality filters on such a table
  found no rows. Write every present bound, like the Java reference.
- json_serde.cc: a CreateTableRequest omitted `stage-create` unless it was
  true. The REST spec leaves the field optional, but Amazon S3 Tables
  rejects a request without it ("stage-create is a required field and
  cannot be null"), so no table could be created there. Always send the
  field, like the Java reference client.
- arrow_io.cc: ArrowFileSystemFileIO::ResolvePath percent-decoded every
  location, through Arrow's URI parser and again explicitly for foreign
  schemes. An Iceberg location is a literal string: the partition path
  percent-encodes partition values, so a data file of the partition
  `k=a b` is the object `.../k=a%20b/...`, and Java's S3URI, PyIceberg and
  DuckDB open that name without decoding it. Keep the text after the scheme
  literal for reading and writing alike, so that a file is stored under
  exactly the location that the table metadata records and is found there.
  A file that a decoding writer stored under the decoded name remains
  readable: reads resolve through ResolveInputPath, which uses the decoded
  name if and only if the filesystem reports (GetFileInfo) that the literal
  name does not exist and that the decoded name is a file. Any other
  answer, including a failed lookup, keeps the literal name, so opening it
  reports the actual error. The lookup happens only for locations that
  contain a percent escape. Writing and deleting never use the decoded
  name.
- incremental_append_scan.cc: an incremental append scan read the manifest
  list of every append snapshot in its range one after the other, also when
  the scan has a plan executor, so a range of several hundred snapshots
  spent minutes there. Read the manifest lists with the plan executor, like
  the manifests themselves. Collect from each list only the manifests that
  its own snapshot added, instead of every manifest that any snapshot of the
  range added: a snapshot's manifest list contains the manifests that the
  snapshot adds and never changes afterwards, so both find the same set, but
  the latter holds each manifest once per later list that still carries it
  (about N * N / 2 entries for N appends) until they are deduplicated.
- manifest_evaluator.cc: a partition field summary without a lower or upper
  bound was taken to mean that all values are null, so the manifest was
  skipped. Writers exist that drop a bound that serializes to zero bytes
  (the empty string), which leaves an inconsistent summary: one bound
  without the other, or no bound while reporting no null and no NaN values.
  Such a summary proves that non-null values exist, so treat the missing
  bound as unknown instead of pruning the manifest. The same writers leave
  no bound and contains_null for a manifest that holds both null and empty
  values, which nothing tells apart from a manifest that holds only null
  values. So for a type whose bound can serialize to zero bytes (string,
  binary, fixed(0)), a summary without bounds never proves that all values
  are null and never prunes; contains_null itself stays reliable, so IsNull
  still prunes on it. For every other type a consistent summary (no bounds
  and contains_null, or only NaN values) prunes as before.

